-- MRCP Part 2: Geriatric medicine (batch 1)
-- 50 questions: continence & functional decline (10), delirium & dementia (10),
-- falls & frailty (10), osteoporosis (10), polypharmacy (10)

  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 81-year-old woman with idiopathic Parkinson's disease (Hoehn and Yahr stage 3) is reviewed for troublesome urgency incontinence, having failed bladder retraining and pelvic floor exercises. She was started on oxybutynin 3mg twice daily four weeks ago. Her family now report she has become increasingly confused and drowsy over the past week, and she has developed a dry mouth and constipation. Her Parkinson's medications have not changed.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in managing her urgency incontinence?',
  array['urgency incontinence',$x$Parkinson's disease$x$,'anticholinergic burden','mirabegron']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 78-year-old man with known benign prostatic hyperplasia presents with a three-day history of constant urinary dribbling and lower abdominal discomfort. He has not been on regular alpha-blocker therapy. Examination reveals a palpable, non-tender suprapubic mass. Bladder scan shows a residual volume of 480ml. Blood tests show creatinine 245 micromol/L (baseline 90 micromol/L three months ago) and potassium 5.6 mmol/L.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['chronic urinary retention','overflow incontinence','post-obstructive AKI','post-obstructive diuresis']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000003', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 85-year-old woman resident in a nursing home has a long-term indwelling urethral catheter for neurogenic bladder dysfunction following a spinal cord infarct. A routine urine dipstick performed as part of a falls assessment shows leucocytes and nitrites, and a subsequent catheter specimen of urine grows Escherichia coli sensitive to trimethoprim. She remains afebrile, has no new confusion, and has no abdominal, flank or suprapubic tenderness.',
  'What is the most appropriate management of the urine culture result?',
  array['catheter-associated bacteriuria','asymptomatic bacteriuria','antimicrobial stewardship']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000004', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 76-year-old woman with stress urinary incontinence secondary to previous vaginal deliveries has completed a supervised three-month course of pelvic floor muscle training with no meaningful improvement. She has multiple comorbidities including poorly controlled atrial fibrillation and severe aortic stenosis, and after discussion with the anaesthetic and urogynaecology teams she is deemed unfit for a surgical continence procedure. She wishes to explore further options to reduce leakage.',
  'What is the most appropriate next pharmacological option?',
  array['stress incontinence','duloxetine','unfit for surgery']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 90-year-old woman in a residential care home develops frequent small-volume episodes of loose, watery faecal soiling over several days. She has chronic constipation and has not had her bowels open normally for over a week. Abdominal examination reveals a firm, non-tender mass in the left iliac fossa, and digital rectal examination reveals a rectum loaded with hard stool.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial management?',
  array['faecal impaction','overflow diarrhoea','spurious diarrhoea','disimpaction']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000006', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 84-year-old man is admitted with community-acquired pneumonia. He is treated appropriately with antibiotics and recovers from the acute infection over five days, but during this admission he has been largely nursed in bed with a urinary catheter in situ for output monitoring that was never removed after his acute kidney injury resolved. By day 7 he is systemically well but has become deconditioned, requiring two people to transfer him, having been independently mobile with a stick prior to admission.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to minimise further functional decline?',
  array['hospital-associated deconditioning','comprehensive geriatric assessment','early mobilisation']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000007', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 79-year-old woman presents with a six-month history of progressive gait disturbance described as a 'magnetic', shuffling gait, new urinary urgency incontinence, and mild but noticeable slowing of her thinking. There is no tremor and no history of stroke. MRI brain shows ventriculomegaly disproportionate to the degree of cortical sulcal atrophy, with no significant white matter ischaemic change.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['normal pressure hydrocephalus','large-volume lumbar puncture','gait apraxia']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000008', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 88-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia is started on tamsulosin for bothersome overflow-type urinary symptoms. He is also taking amlodipine and ramipril for hypertension. Two hours after his first dose, he experiences dizziness and is found by staff to have fallen while standing from his chair, with a lying-standing blood pressure drop of 30/15 mmHg.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['alpha-blocker','first-dose hypotension','orthostatic hypotension','falls']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000009', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 83-year-old woman with chronic heart failure is on furosemide 40mg, which she takes at 8pm as this fits her routine. She reports waking three to four times per night to pass large volumes of urine, resulting in disturbed sleep and two recent falls when getting up in the dark. Her heart failure is otherwise well controlled with no evidence of decompensation, and her daytime blood pressure and renal function are stable.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial change to reduce her falls risk?',
  array['nocturia','diuretic timing','nocturnal polyuria','falls prevention']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000010', 'mrcp2', '96d01c15-e03f-4c99-956d-44b37e38fdbb',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 77-year-old woman describes both involuntary leakage on coughing and lifting (worse than her urgency symptoms), together with occasional urgency-related leakage before reaching the toilet. Urodynamic studies confirm mixed urinary incontinence with a predominant stress component. She has already completed supervised pelvic floor muscle training with only modest benefit in her urge symptoms and no change in her stress leakage, which she finds far more bothersome and socially limiting.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['mixed urinary incontinence','predominant symptom','individualised management']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000011', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 84-year-old woman is admitted with a lower respiratory tract infection and develops hyperactive delirium on day two, becoming agitated, trying to climb out of bed and pulling at her intravenous cannula, placing herself and staff at risk of injury. Reversible contributing factors (hypoxia, constipation, urinary retention, pain, and drug causes) have been actively assessed and addressed, and verbal de-escalation and reorientation strategies have been attempted without success. She has no history of Parkinsonism, dementia with Lewy bodies, or prolonged QT interval.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in managing her agitation?',
  array['hyperactive delirium','haloperidol','non-pharmacological measures failed']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000012', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 80-year-old man with a two-year history of dementia with Lewy bodies, characterised by fluctuating cognition, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations and Parkinsonian features, is admitted with worsening agitation and paranoid ideation that is distressing him. Non-pharmacological measures and treatment of any acute precipitant have not fully controlled his distress, and his family and the ward team feel some pharmacological treatment is now needed for his safety and wellbeing.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to antipsychotic use in this patient?',
  array['dementia with Lewy bodies','neuroleptic sensitivity','antipsychotic caution']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000013', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 79-year-old woman with mild Alzheimer's disease was started on donepezil 5mg daily eight weeks ago, increased to 10mg four weeks ago. She now presents with two episodes of pre-syncope and is found to have a resting heart rate of 42 beats per minute. ECG shows a new first-degree atrioventricular block progressing to occasional second-degree (Mobitz type I) block on telemetry. She is not on any beta-blocker or other rate-limiting medication.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate immediate management?',
  array['donepezil','bradycardia','cholinesterase inhibitor cardiac effects']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000014', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 86-year-old man with moderate vascular dementia has developed increasingly severe agitation, verbal aggression towards carers, and attempts to strike staff during personal care, causing significant distress and risk of injury to himself and others. A structured behavioural assessment has identified no clear environmental trigger or physical cause (pain, infection, constipation), and person-centred non-pharmacological approaches over the past three weeks, delivered consistently, have not reduced the frequency or severity of these episodes.',
  'What is the most appropriate pharmacological option, and for how long should it be used?',
  array['behavioural and psychological symptoms of dementia','risperidone','antipsychotic stroke risk']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000015', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 90-year-old woman with moderate dementia is noted on medication review to be taking oxybutynin for urinary urgency, amitriptyline 10mg at night that was started years ago for neuropathic pain, and chlorphenamine as required for itch. Her family report that her memory and alertness have appeared to worsen gradually over recent months, coinciding with the period these medications have all been in regular use together.',
  'What is the most appropriate next step in her management?',
  array['anticholinergic burden','deprescribing','cognitive decline']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000016', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 76-year-old woman is recovering on the orthopaedic ward on day 3 after surgical fixation of a hip fracture. She becomes acutely agitated and disoriented overnight. On assessment she is found to have not opened her bowels for five days, has not passed urine for 10 hours despite adequate intravenous fluids, and her regular co-codamol has been given regularly for pain that she is unable to clearly localise or describe.',
  'What is the most appropriate first step in her management?',
  array['postoperative delirium','reversible precipitants','urinary retention','constipation']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000017', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 74-year-old man with known alcohol dependence and compensated liver cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B) is admitted following a fall. On day two of admission, having had no alcohol, he develops tremor, sweating, tachycardia, and visual hallucinations consistent with alcohol withdrawal progressing towards delirium tremens. His most recent liver function tests show albumin 28 g/L and INR 1.6.',
  'What is the most appropriate choice of benzodiazepine for symptom-triggered withdrawal treatment?',
  array['alcohol withdrawal','delirium tremens','hepatic impairment','lorazepam']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000018', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 68-year-old man presents with a three-year history of progressive personality change, disinhibition, and loss of empathy noted by his family, with relatively preserved episodic memory and visuospatial function on formal cognitive testing. MRI brain shows focal frontal and anterior temporal lobe atrophy. A diagnosis of behavioural variant frontotemporal dementia is made, and his family ask about starting a medication to slow his cognitive decline, having heard about drugs used for Alzheimer's disease.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate advice regarding cholinesterase inhibitors and memantine in this condition?',
  array['frontotemporal dementia','cholinesterase inhibitors not indicated','behavioural management']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000019', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$An 88-year-old woman with moderate Alzheimer's disease is admitted with delirium secondary to a confirmed urinary tract infection, treated with an appropriate course of antibiotics. Her infective markers normalise and her urinary symptoms resolve, but four days into treatment she remains intermittently agitated, disoriented, and is trying to leave the ward. A colleague suggests starting regular risperidone.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step before considering an antipsychotic?',
  array['delirium superimposed on dementia','reassessment','resolving precipitant']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000020', 'mrcp2', '4b42bd2e-ab19-4388-abef-108a48c7a6cd',
  'draft', 'core',
  $x$A 77-year-old man with mild-to-moderate Alzheimer's disease has been established on donepezil 10mg daily for six months with good cognitive benefit, but reports persistent nausea and occasional vomiting that is affecting his appetite and quality of life. Simple measures (taking the dose with food, and reassurance) have not helped. His family are keen to continue treatment given the cognitive benefit he has shown.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate next step in management?',
  array['cholinesterase inhibitor GI side effects','rivastigmine patch','formulation switch']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000021', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 82-year-old woman is referred to the falls clinic after three falls in the past two months. She takes amlodipine, bendroflumethiazide, tamsulosin, and citalopram. Lying and standing blood pressure shows a drop from 148/82 mmHg lying to 108/64 mmHg after two minutes standing, reproducing her presyncopal symptoms. Cardiac and neurological examination are otherwise unremarkable, and her ECG shows normal sinus rhythm.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial approach to her management?',
  array['orthostatic hypotension','medication review','falls prevention','deprescribing']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000022', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 91-year-old man with severe frailty (Clinical Frailty Scale 8, living in a nursing home with total dependency for personal care) is admitted with severe community-acquired pneumonia and type 1 respiratory failure. His family and the medical team are discussing the appropriate ceiling of treatment, including whether intensive care unit admission would be appropriate should he deteriorate further.',
  'What is the most appropriate use of the Clinical Frailty Scale in this decision?',
  array['Clinical Frailty Scale','ceiling of care','shared decision-making']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000023', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 84-year-old man presents with recurrent unexplained falls, sometimes associated with a brief loss of consciousness, occurring particularly when his shirt collar is tight or when turning his head while shaving. Carotid sinus massage under continuous ECG and blood pressure monitoring produces a 4.2-second asystolic pause with minimal associated blood pressure drop, reproducing his symptoms.',
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['carotid sinus hypersensitivity','cardioinhibitory type','permanent pacemaker']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000024', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 79-year-old woman presents with recurrent brief episodes of vertigo lasting seconds, triggered by rolling over in bed and looking upwards to reach a high shelf, without hearing loss, tinnitus, or other neurological symptoms. The Dix-Hallpike manoeuvre reproduces her vertigo with a brief upbeating-torsional nystagmus that fatigues on repeated testing.',
  'What is the most appropriate first-line treatment?',
  array['benign paroxysmal positional vertigo','Epley manoeuvre','falls prevention']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000025', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 85-year-old woman meets criteria for physical frailty: unintentional weight loss of 6kg over the past year, self-reported exhaustion, low grip strength on dynamometry, slow gait speed, and low physical activity levels. She lives independently but has had two recent falls and is increasingly anxious about further decline.',
  'What is the most effective evidence-based intervention to address her underlying frailty?',
  array['frailty phenotype','resistance exercise','protein intake']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000026', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 87-year-old woman who fell three weeks ago sustaining only minor bruising has since become markedly deconditioned, reporting a persistent fear of falling that has led her to avoid walking outside the home and reduce her overall activity. She is otherwise medically stable, with no new orthostatic hypotension, cardiac, or neurological findings on a comprehensive falls assessment.',
  'What is the most appropriate intervention to address her fear of falling and reduce future falls risk?',
  array['fear of falling','post-fall syndrome','strength and balance training']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000027', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 78-year-old man with Parkinson's disease on co-careldopa and a dopamine agonist reports increasing falls over recent weeks, occurring particularly on standing from sitting. Lying-standing blood pressure confirms a symptomatic drop of 30/15 mmHg. His motor symptoms are otherwise reasonably controlled, though he has noticed his dopamine agonist dose was recently increased for wearing-off symptoms.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate initial approach to managing his falls?',
  array[$x$Parkinson's disease$x$,'orthostatic hypotension','dopamine agonist','medication review']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000028', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 83-year-old woman with permanent atrial fibrillation (CHA2DS2-VASc score 5) is established on warfarin with a stable INR. She has had four unwitnessed falls in the past year, none resulting in significant injury, and her family and general practitioner are considering whether her anticoagulation should be stopped because of her falls risk. She has no history of intracranial haemorrhage and no other high bleeding risk features.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her anticoagulation?',
  array['anticoagulation','falls risk','atrial fibrillation','risk-benefit reassessment']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000029', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 76-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus on gliclazide has had two falls in the past month, both associated with sweating, tremor and confusion that resolved after eating. Capillary glucose recorded by paramedics during one episode was 2.8 mmol/L. He lives with moderate frailty and mild cognitive impairment, and his most recent HbA1c is 48 mmol/mol (6.5%).',
  'What is the most appropriate change to his diabetes management?',
  array['hypoglycaemia','sulfonylurea','frailty-adjusted glycaemic targets','deprescribing']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000030', 'mrcp2', '9b117d1b-017b-44e7-9dbc-f84a59007c28',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'An 89-year-old woman is found on comprehensive geriatric assessment to have lost 5kg unintentionally over the past year, reports feeling exhausted most days, has a grip strength in the lowest quintile for her age and sex, and walks at 0.6 metres per second, with low overall physical activity. She has multiple chronic conditions and takes eight regular medications. Her family are concerned about her increasing frequency of hospital admissions.',
  'Which assessment finding pattern best supports a diagnosis of frailty (as opposed to disability or comorbidity alone) in this woman?',
  array['frailty phenotype','Fried criteria','comprehensive geriatric assessment']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000031', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 74-year-old woman has been taking oral alendronic acid for postmenopausal osteoporosis for five years, having sustained a wrist fracture prior to starting treatment. She has had no further fractures, her most recent DXA scan shows a femoral neck T-score of -2.1, and she has no new risk factors. She asks whether she needs to continue treatment indefinitely.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her ongoing management?',
  array['bisphosphonate holiday','treatment duration','fracture risk reassessment']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000032', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  $x$A 71-year-old woman who has taken oral alendronic acid for nine years presents with several weeks of dull, aching left thigh pain, occurring spontaneously with minimal or no trauma. Plain radiograph shows a transverse fracture line with focal cortical thickening ('beaking') on the lateral cortex of the femoral shaft, without comminution.$x$,
  'What is the most appropriate management?',
  array['atypical femoral fracture','bisphosphonate-related complication','teriparatide']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000033', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 68-year-old woman with postmenopausal osteoporosis has been taking oral alendronic acid for 18 months. She requires a routine, straightforward extraction of a lower molar tooth for dental caries. She has no history of head and neck radiotherapy, malignancy, or intravenous bisphosphonate use, and has good oral hygiene.',
  'What is the most appropriate advice regarding her osteoporosis treatment around the time of dental extraction?',
  array['oral bisphosphonates','medication-related osteonecrosis of the jaw','dental extraction']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000034', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 79-year-old woman sustains a hip fracture after a low-impact fall. DXA performed after fixation shows a femoral neck T-score of -3.4. She has also had two previous vertebral fragility fractures. She has never previously received osteoporosis treatment.',
  'What is the most appropriate initial pharmacological approach to her osteoporosis?',
  array['very high fracture risk','anabolic-first strategy','sequential therapy']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000035', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 75-year-old woman has been treated with denosumab injections every six months for severe osteoporosis for four years, with good response on DXA. Her most recent injection, due three months ago, has been missed because of a house move and administrative delay, and she now asks about restarting treatment.',
  'What is the most important consideration in planning her ongoing management?',
  array['denosumab discontinuation','rebound vertebral fractures','bisphosphonate bridging']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000036', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 80-year-old woman with osteoporosis and stage 3b chronic kidney disease (eGFR 32 ml/min/1.73m2) is found to have a corrected calcium of 2.05 mmol/L (low-normal) and a vitamin D level of 28 nmol/L (deficient) before planned initiation of osteoporosis treatment.',
  'What is the most appropriate step before starting antiresorptive therapy?',
  array['vitamin D deficiency','hypocalcaemia risk','denosumab','pre-treatment optimisation']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000037', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 73-year-old woman with polymyalgia rheumatica has been started on prednisolone 20mg daily, with an anticipated total treatment duration exceeding 12 months. She has no prior fragility fracture. Her FRAX-based fracture risk assessment (incorporating glucocorticoid use) places her in a high-risk category.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to bone protection?',
  array['glucocorticoid-induced osteoporosis','FRAX','bone protection without waiting for DXA']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000038', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 77-year-old woman with osteoporosis and a background of a Nissen fundoplication with a residual oesophageal stricture has been unable to tolerate oral alendronic acid due to severe reflux and difficulty swallowing tablets, despite trying appropriate administration technique.',
  'What is the most appropriate alternative treatment?',
  array['oral bisphosphonate intolerance','oesophageal disease','intravenous zoledronic acid']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000039', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 76-year-old woman has osteoporosis with a hip T-score of -2.9 and stage 4 chronic kidney disease (eGFR 22 ml/min/1.73m2). Her corrected calcium and vitamin D levels are within the normal range following recent optimisation. Her nephrologist and geriatrician are discussing which pharmacological agent would be most appropriate for her osteoporosis.',
  'What is the most appropriate treatment choice?',
  array['chronic kidney disease','bisphosphonate contraindication','denosumab','hypocalcaemia monitoring']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000040', 'mrcp2', '1ca0d43c-0d3f-43d1-bdf1-743673dd2bf4',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 69-year-old woman is investigated for low-trauma vertebral fractures found incidentally on a chest radiograph. Blood tests show corrected calcium 2.78 mmol/L, phosphate 0.68 mmol/L, and parathyroid hormone markedly elevated. A subsequent sestamibi scan confirms a solitary parathyroid adenoma. Her eGFR is normal and she has no nephrolithiasis.',
  'What is the most appropriate definitive management of her bone disease?',
  array['primary hyperparathyroidism','secondary osteoporosis','parathyroidectomy']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000041', 'mrcp2', '5980fc24-6fba-4c39-bc8f-954dc05595dd',
  'draft', 'core',
  'An 84-year-old man with hypertension and osteoarthritis, established on ramipril and bendroflumethiazide, is started by his GP on regular ibuprofen for knee pain. One week later he is admitted with lethargy, reduced urine output, and blood tests showing creatinine risen from a baseline of 95 to 310 micromol/L, with potassium 5.9 mmol/L.',
  'What is the most appropriate immediate step in his medication management?',
  array['triple whammy','NSAID-induced AKI','ACE inhibitor','diuretic']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000042', 'mrcp2', '5980fc24-6fba-4c39-bc8f-954dc05595dd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 90-year-old woman with mild cognitive impairment is reviewed in a medicines optimisation clinic. Her regular medications include oxybutynin for urgency incontinence, amitriptyline 10mg at night for chronic neuropathic pain, cyclizine as required for nausea, and chlorphenamine as required for hay fever. Her Anticholinergic Burden score is calculated as significantly elevated, and her family report worsening confusion and two recent falls.',
  'What is the most appropriate overarching approach to her medication review?',
  array['anticholinergic burden score','deprescribing','cumulative drug burden']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000043', 'mrcp2', '5980fc24-6fba-4c39-bc8f-954dc05595dd',
  'draft', 'core',
  'A 77-year-old man has taken regular diazepam 5mg three times daily for over ten years, originally started for anxiety following a bereavement. He and his family now wish for this to be stopped, citing concerns about long-term sedation and falls risk. He has no acute indication requiring the medication currently.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to discontinuing his diazepam?',
  array['benzodiazepine deprescribing','gradual dose reduction','withdrawal risk']
),
('d5000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000044', 'mrcp2', '5980fc24-6fba-4c39-bc8f-954dc05595dd',
  'draft', 'advanced',
  'A 92-year-old woman with severe frailty, advanced dementia, and a life expectancy estimated in months due to end-stage heart failure, is taking atorvastatin 40mg for secondary prevention following a myocardial infarction 15 years ago. Her family ask whether all of her medications are still necessary, given her limited prognosis and the burden of her current 11-medication regimen.',
  'What is the most appropriate approach to her statin therapy?',
  array['deprescribing','statin','limited life expectancy','goals of care']
),
